Apple's Car Keys feature appears to be coming to supported vehicles made by Chinese maker Xiaomi Auto, based on code changes discovered by <em>MacRumors</em> in Apple's Wallet app backend.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2025/07/digital-car-key-iphone.jpg" alt="" width="1600" height="900" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1010336" /><br/>
Code spotted in the third developer beta of iOS 27 back in <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/07/08/ios-27-car-key-support-for-lucid-and-xiaomi/">July</a> revealed that Apple was preparing Car Keys support for Xiaomi, so this latest change suggest support is imminent. <br/>
<br/>
The Chinese electronics maker has expanded into EVs with models like the SU7 sedan and YU7 SUV. Car key support would be one of the company's first tie-ins with Apple's ecosystem, despite Xiaomi being a major Apple rival in the smartphone market.<br/>
<br/>
Car Keys allows an iPhone or Apple Watch with NFC capabilities to unlock a vehicle through the Wallet app. A digital version of a car key is stored in Wallet, and unlocking can be done simply by holding an Apple Watch or ‌iPhone‌ near a compatible vehicle's NFC reader.<br/>
<br/>
What can be done with Car Keys may vary by car manufacturer, but at a minimum, Car Keys can be used to unlock your car, lock your car, and start your car, which are the features available with a physical key.<br/>
<br/>
